### What are the differences between a Stream and a Collection?

### Comparison table

| Aspect | Collection | Stream |
|--------|-----------|--------|
| Storage | Stores elements in memory | Stores nothing |
| Evaluation | Eager: all elements exist | Lazy: computed on demand |
| Reuse | Reusable: iterate many times | Single-use: consumed after terminal |
| Mutability | Add, remove, modify | Cannot modify source |
| Size | Known: size() method | Unknown: no size() |
| Random access | Yes: get(index) | No: sequential or parallel |
| Iteration | External (your loop) | Internal (stream controls) |
| Parallelism | Manual threads | Built-in parallelStream() |

### Code comparison

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class StreamVsCollection {
    record Account(String id, BigDecimal balance, boolean active) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // COLLECTION: stores, accesses, modifies
        List<Account> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000"), true),
            new Account("SAV2002", new BigDecimal("8000"), false),
            new Account("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("250000"), true)
        ));
        System.out.println("Size: " + accounts.size());     // 3
        System.out.println("First: " + accounts.get(0).id()); // SAV2001

        // STREAM: processes, transforms, aggregates
        BigDecimal activeTotal = accounts.stream()
            .filter(Account::active)
            .map(Account::balance)
            .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
        System.out.println("Active total: Rs." + activeTotal);

        Map<Boolean, List<Account>> partitioned = accounts.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(Account::active));
        System.out.println("Active: " + partitioned.get(true).size());
    }
}
```

```text
Size: 3
First: SAV2001
Active total: Rs.375000
Active: 2
```

### How do they work together?

The typical pattern is Collection -> Stream -> pipeline -> collect() -> Collection.

```java
// JPA returns a Collection from the database
List<Account> accounts = repository.findAll();

// Process with Stream
List<String> activeIds = accounts.stream()
    .filter(Account::active)
    .map(Account::id)
    .sorted()
    .toList();

// Return as DTO
return new ReportDTO(activeIds);
```

### Why are streams single-use?

```java
import java.util.stream.Stream;

Stream<String> ids = accounts.stream().map(Account::id);
ids.count(); // terminal operation consumes the stream

// WRONG: reusing consumed stream
// ids.toList(); // IllegalStateException: stream has already been operated upon!

// CORRECT: create a new stream
accounts.stream().map(Account::id).toList();
```

### Why is laziness important?

Lazy evaluation means intermediate operations build a pipeline but do not process any data until a terminal operation triggers it. This enables short-circuiting.

```java
// Without laziness: filter ALL, then find first
// With laziness: stop as soon as the first match passes
Optional<Account> first = accounts.stream()
    .filter(Account::active)
    .findFirst(); // stops at the first active account
```

### What are the iteration differences?

```java
// EXTERNAL iteration (Collection): you control the loop
for (Account a : accounts) {
    if (a.active()) { System.out.println(a.id()); }
}

// INTERNAL iteration (Stream): stream controls traversal
accounts.stream()
    .filter(Account::active)
    .map(Account::id)
    .forEach(System.out::println);
```

Internal iteration allows the stream to optimise traversal order and parallelise transparently.

### What about mutability?

```java
// Collection: supports add, remove, set
accounts.add(new Account("FD5001", new BigDecimal("500000"), true));
accounts.remove(0);

// Stream: cannot modify the source
// accounts.stream().forEach(a -> accounts.remove(a)); // ConcurrentModificationException!
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Collections and Streams are complementary. Neither replaces the other.
- Use Collection when you need to store, index, search, or modify data.
- Use Stream when you need to filter, transform, aggregate, or parallelise processing.
- In a typical Spring service, JPA returns a Collection, you process with Streams, and return the result as a DTO.
- Stream operations do not modify the source. The original Collection remains the single source of truth.
